# Matching provenance and consumer correction, 2026-09-13

The existing common operands express the source lookup completely when the selected world and
anchor accompany the matching endpoint. The [working procedure](../research/perception-matching-trace/0.1.0/README.md)
traces ten checked matching pairs to their world-specific reference records and original
detector rows. No production code, common schema or review protocol changes were needed.

The initial question was whether sharing an object across worlds lost a usable source link.
The existing full-context manual join was the comparator. The bounded synthetic example has
two worlds and two anchors, with one graph node naming four different source record digests.
Its unchanged finite comparison is [-1,1]. The replay checks all four unique shared-node lookups, original
prediction file and sample-array identities, zero-based row indices, proposal members, phases,
capture identities and two delivered images against their original bytes. Incomplete joins and
a substituted other-world digest are refused; equal geometry cannot justify merging provenance.

The exercise also exposes the limit of the supplied records. Each proposal names a whole image
capture, without a point or pixel locator, and the capture precedes its declared reference
position by two seconds. The trace preserves both facts. It proves no object association,
motion reconstruction, viewing event, human identity, independent judgment or physical coverage.
All source and review records in this example are explicitly synthetic engineering fixtures.

The source preparation and first trace process took about 1.11 and 0.48 seconds respectively
on the selected local runtime. Preparation includes generating synthetic sources, admission,
assistance and common operands; the trace reconstructs the common packet and verifies its
decision before following links. Final measured runs and exact output digests are in
[verification](../research/perception-matching-trace/0.1.0/verification.json).
These are single-process observations, not a speed comparison or human-effort measurement.
Investigation, preparation, code review, verification, documentation and integration time are
retained separately in the private checkpoint costs. No participant effort was measured.

